.\" Process this file with:
.\" mandoc -a aethercli.1
.\" or:
.\" man ./aethercli.1
.TH AETHERCLI 1 "2026-10-30" "aethercli 0.7.0" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
aethercli \- interactive CLI interface and command dispatcher for automation
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBaethercli\fR [\fB\-C\fR \fIconfig_file\fR] [\fB\-l\fR \fIlang\fR] [\fB\-\-log\fR [\fIpath\fR]]
.br
\fBaethercli\fR \fB\-p\fR "\fIcommand\fR" [\fB\-C\fR \fIconfig_file\fR] [\fB\-l\fR \fIlang\fR] [\fB\-\-log\fR [\fIpath\fR]]
.br
\fBaethercli\fR \fB\-\-mcp\fR [\fB\-C\fR \fIconfig_file\fR] [\fB\-l\fR \fIlang\fR] [\fB\-\-log\fR [\fIpath\fR]]
.br
\fBaethercli\fR \fB\-\-adduser\fR \fIname\fR [\fB\-C\fR \fIconfig_file\fR]
.br
\fBaethercli\fR \fB\-h\fR | \fB\-\-help\fR
.br
\fBaethercli\fR \fB\-v\fR | \fB\-\-version\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBaethercli\fR is a command-line terminal utility designed to act as a
unified, ergonomic, and auditable interface layer for system administration
and automation tasks.
Inspired by network appliance operating consoles (such as Cisco IOS),
\fBaethercli\fR features real-time interactive navigation, TAB completion,
instant option queries via question mark (\fB?\fR), persistent history,
and strict parameter validation prior to dispatching commands to underlying
operating system binaries or scripts.

The tool is data-driven: its command tree is entirely defined in JSON format
(\fBconfig.json\fR). The binary is application-agnostic, making it ideal
for shared, static, system-wide installation while individual applications
or services maintain their own dedicated configuration files and automation scripts.

.SH READ-ONLY INSTALLATION (SHARED SYSTEM ACCESS)
The recommended deployment architecture is to install the compiled
\fBaethercli\fR binary into a global system directory (such as
\fI/usr/local/bin\fR) with ownership assigned to the superuser (\fBroot\fR)
and permissions restricted to read-and-execute for regular users (\fB0755\fR).
This allows all users and system services to run the tool while preventing
unprivileged users from tampering with, replacing, or modifying the binary.

.SS 1. Source compilation
Compile the project using C++17 and release optimizations:
.PP
.nf
mkdir \-p build
cd build
cmake \-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \-DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/usr/local ..
make \-j$(nproc 2>/dev/null || sysctl \-n hw.ncpu 2>/dev/null || echo 2)
.fi

.SS 2. Installation and strict permission assignment
Execute the installation as \fBroot\fR:
.PP
.nf
sudo make install
.fi
.PP
Next, verify that binary ownership and access permissions guarantee execution
by all users and write access strictly restricted to root:
.PP
.nf
# Set owner to root
sudo chown root:root /usr/local/bin/aethercli 2>/dev/null || \\
    sudo chown root:wheel /usr/local/bin/aethercli

# Mode 0755: rwxr-xr-x (global read and execute; write for root only)
sudo chmod 0755 /usr/local/bin/aethercli
.fi
.PP
If global shared configuration files are stored in \fI/usr/local/etc/aethercli\fR,
ensure they also have strict read-only permissions:
.PP
.nf
sudo chmod 0755 /usr/local/etc/aethercli
sudo chmod 0644 /usr/local/etc/aethercli/config.json 2>/dev/null || true
sudo chmod 0755 /usr/local/etc/aethercli/scripts 2>/dev/null || true
sudo chmod 0755 /usr/local/etc/aethercli/scripts/*.sh 2>/dev/null || true
.fi

.SH AUTOMATION ARCHITECTURE: THE WRAPPER SCRIPT PATTERN
To manage multiple applications, background services, or automation jobs on a
single server, do not combine them into a single monolithic configuration.
Instead, adopt the \fIWrapper Script Pattern\fR.

The workflow operates as follows:
.IP \(bu 3
The wrapper script resides in an executable directory in \fB$PATH\fR
(for example, \fI/usr/local/bin/<app-name>\fR).
.IP \(bu 3
The script points to the application's dedicated local \fBconfig.json\fR
(for example, \fI/opt/<app>/etc/config.json\fR).
.IP \(bu 3
The script executes \fI/usr/local/bin/aethercli\fR passing the \fB\-C\fR option
with the configuration path and forwarding all command-line arguments (\fB"$@"\fR).

.SS Security Considerations for Wrapper Scripts
IMPORTANT: Each application's \fBconfig.json\fR and its enclosing directory
must be owned by \fBroot\fR with permissions \fB0644\fR (and \fB0755\fR for
directories). In \fBaethercli\fR, whoever can edit \fBconfig.json\fR controls
what commands are executed on the host. Allowing non-root users write access
to the configuration creates an arbitrary command execution vector.

.SS Wrapper Script Example
Create \fI/usr/local/bin/backup-cli\fR:
.PP
.nf
#!/bin/sh
# Automation wrapper for backup services using aethercli
set \-e

CONF="/opt/backup-system/etc/config.json"

if [ ! \-r "$CONF" ]; then
    echo "Error: config file $CONF not found or not readable." >&2
    exit 1
fi

exec /usr/local/bin/aethercli \-C "$CONF" "$@"
.fi
.PP
Grant execute permissions:
.PP
.nf
sudo chown root:root /usr/local/bin/backup-cli 2>/dev/null || true
sudo chmod 0755 /usr/local/bin/backup-cli
.fi
.PP
With this wrapper, operators and automated scripts can use:
.PP
.nf
# Interactive mode with TAB completion and contextual help:
backup-cli

# Non-interactive headless automation (cron, CI/CD):
backup-cli \-p "database backup"
.fi

.SH CONFIGURATION METHODOLOGY: THE OBJECT-ACTION PARADIGM
When designing configuration files for \fBaethercli\fR, always follow the
\fBObject-Action\fR paradigm.

.SS The Problem with the Action-First (Verb-First) Model
When a CLI is structured with verbs at the root level (e.g., \fBstart-service\fR,
\fBstop-service\fR, \fBbackup-db\fR, \fBcheck-disk\fR), the root autocomplete
namespace becomes cluttered and confusing. Operators must remember which verb
was selected by the developer, and the top-level option tree lacks cohesion.

.SS The Object-Action Solution
In the Object-Action model, the first level always identifies the \fBObject\fR
(the resource or entity to act upon). The second level specifies the \fBAction\fR
(the operation to execute on that object).

.IP \(bu 3
\fBLevel 1 (Object):\fR \fIservice\fR, \fIdatabase\fR, \fInetwork\fR, \fIsystem\fR, \fIconfig\fR.
.IP \(bu 3
\fBLevel 2 (Action):\fR \fIstart\fR, \fIstop\fR, \fIrestart\fR, \fIstatus\fR, \fIbackup\fR, \fIreload\fR.
.IP \(bu 3
\fBLevel 3 / Arguments:\fR specific instance identifiers, table names, or flags.

.SS Why Object-Action Simplifies Configuration:
.IP \(bu 3
\fBModularity and Grouping:\fR Each entity forms an independent JSON block.
Adding a new action to an object requires merely adding an entry to its
\fBsubcommands\fR array.
.IP \(bu 3
\fBGuided Discovery:\fR Typing \fBdatabase ?\fR immediately lists only
actions applicable to databases.
.IP \(bu 3
\fBCollision-Free Verbs:\fR Common action names such as \fBstatus\fR, \fBstart\fR,
or \fBlist\fR can co-exist under different objects without naming conflicts
(\fBservice status\fR vs. \fBdatabase status\fR).
.IP \(bu 3
\fBEfficient Autocompletion:\fR Reduces top-level ambiguity when pressing TAB.

.SH CONFIGURATION FILE STRUCTURE (JSON)
Configuration files use standard JSON formatting.

.SS Global Properties
.TP
.B "motd" \fR(string, optional)
Welcome banner printed at the start of an interactive session.
.TP
.B "config_version" \fR(string, optional)
Version string of the configuration displayed in the header.
.TP
.B "scripts_dir" \fR(string, optional)
Base directory for external activation scripts. If relative, it is resolved
against the directory containing \fBconfig.json\fR.
.TP
.B "language" \fR(string, optional)
Language code (\fBen\fR or \fBpt\fR). Default is \fBen\fR.
.TP
.B "status_bar" \fR(boolean, optional)
If \fBtrue\fR, enables the real-time VT100 metrics status bar at terminal bottom.
.TP
.B "require_authentication" \fR(boolean, optional)
Whether authentication against \fBusers.json\fR is required at startup.
.TP
.B "restricted_session" \fR(boolean, optional)
If \fBtrue\fR, prevents multiple concurrent active sessions for the same user via POSIX file locks.
.TP
.B "number_auth_fail" \fR(integer, optional)
Maximum allowed failed authentication attempts before disconnect (default: 3).
.TP
.B "passwd-file" \fR(string, optional)
Path to cryptographic user hashes (\fBusers.json\fR).
.TP
.B "commands" \fR(array, required)
List of top-level command objects in the hierarchy.

.SS Command / Subcommand Properties
.TP
.B "name" \fR(string, required)
Text token entered at the prompt.
.TP
.B "abbrev" \fR(string, optional)
Explicit canonical abbreviation for the command.
.TP
.B "short_desc" \fR(string, optional)
Brief summary displayed during double-TAB completion and help listings.
.TP
.B "description" \fR(string, optional)
Extended description or documentation.
.TP
.B "syntax" \fR(string, optional)
Syntax guidance shown in triple-TAB help.
.TP
.B "activation" \fR(string, optional on intermediate parent nodes)
OS command line, external script, or reserved engine directive
(\fBinternal:reload\fR, \fBinternal:run_macro\fR, \fBinternal:config_mode\fR).
.TP
.B "admin_only" \fR(boolean, optional)
If \fBtrue\fR, restricts the command and everything below it to the authenticated "admin" user.
.TP
.B "args" \fR(string, default "none")
When set to \fB"append"\fR, extra arguments typed by the user are sanitized
and forwarded to the activation command. When set to \fB"none"\fR, unconfigured
trailing arguments are rejected with an error.
.TP
.B "timeout_s" \fR(integer, optional)
Maximum run time in seconds. The whole process group gets SIGTERM, then SIGKILL.
Exit status 124 in headless mode.
.TP
.B "max_output_bytes" \fR(integer, optional)
Per-stream output limit; excess output is discarded with a warning. In MCP mode,
commands that do not set it are limited to 10 MiB.
.TP
.B "cwd" \fR(string, optional)
Working directory. If relative, resolved against the directory of \fBconfig.json\fR.
.TP
.B "env" \fR(object, optional)
Environment variables for the command.
.TP
.B "params" \fR(array of objects, optional)
Typed positional parameters; mutually exclusive with \fB"args": "append"\fR.
Fields: \fBname\fR (letters, digits and underscore, not starting with a digit),
\fBtype\fR (\fBstring\fR, \fBinteger\fR, \fBboolean\fR), \fBrequired\fR,
\fBenum\fR, \fBpattern\fR (ECMAScript; the whole value must match),
\fBminimum\fR, \fBmaximum\fR, \fBdescription\fR.
Values arrive as arguments and as \fBAETHERCLI_PARAM_\fINAME\fR variables.
.TP
.B "effect" \fR(string, default "write")
\fBread\fR, \fBwrite\fR or \fBdestructive\fR; published as MCP tool annotations.
.TP
.B "idempotent" \fR(boolean, default false), \fB"open_world"\fR (boolean, default true)
Published as the MCP annotations \fBidempotentHint\fR and \fBopenWorldHint\fR.
.TP
.B "subcommands" \fR(array of objects, optional)
Nested list of child actions or sub-resources.

.SH COMPLETE OBJECT-ACTION CONFIGURATION EXAMPLE
The following is a complete, valid \fBconfig.json\fR adhering strictly to
the Object-Action model:

.PP
.nf
{
  "motd": "Service Operations Center\\nType '?' for available commands.\\n",
  "config_version": "1.0",
  "scripts_dir": "scripts",
  "require_authentication": false,
  "status_bar": false,
  "commands": [
    {
      "name": "service",
      "short_desc": "Service lifecycle management",
      "subcommands": [
        {
          "name": "start",
          "short_desc": "Start a background service",
          "activation": "systemctl start --",
          "syntax": "service start <name>",
          "args": "append"
        },
        {
          "name": "stop",
          "short_desc": "Stop a background service",
          "activation": "systemctl stop --",
          "syntax": "service stop <name>",
          "args": "append"
        },
        {
          "name": "restart",
          "short_desc": "Restart a background service",
          "activation": "systemctl restart --",
          "syntax": "service restart <name>",
          "args": "append"
        },
        {
          "name": "status",
          "short_desc": "Check execution state of a service",
          "activation": "systemctl status --",
          "syntax": "service status <name>",
          "args": "append"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "database",
      "short_desc": "Database operations",
      "subcommands": [
        {
          "name": "backup",
          "short_desc": "Create full database backup",
          "activation": "${AETHERCLI_SCRIPTS_DIR}/db-backup.sh",
          "syntax": "database backup"
        },
        {
          "name": "status",
          "short_desc": "Show connections and health",
          "activation": "${AETHERCLI_SCRIPTS_DIR}/db-status.sh",
          "syntax": "database status"
        },
        {
          "name": "optimize",
          "short_desc": "Run maintenance routines",
          "activation": "${AETHERCLI_SCRIPTS_DIR}/db-optimize.sh",
          "syntax": "database optimize"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "system",
      "short_desc": "System resource monitoring",
      "subcommands": [
        {
          "name": "disk",
          "short_desc": "Filesystem usage",
          "activation": "df \-h",
          "syntax": "system disk"
        },
        {
          "name": "memory",
          "short_desc": "Host memory consumption",
          "activation": "free \-m 2>/dev/null || vm_stat",
          "syntax": "system memory"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "config",
      "short_desc": "AetherCLI configuration actions",
      "subcommands": [
        {
          "name": "reload",
          "short_desc": "Reload config.json and translations live",
          "activation": "internal:reload",
          "syntax": "config reload"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
.fi

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-C\fR \fIfile\fR, \fB\-\-conf\fR \fIfile\fR
Specify the JSON configuration file to load. If omitted, falls back to
\fI~/.aethercli/config.json\fR or the compiled-in default path.
.TP
\fB\-p\fR "\fIcommand\fR"
Headless non-interactive mode. Executes the given command string, prints output,
and terminates with the command's exit code.
.TP
\fB\-l\fR \fIlang\fR, \fB\-\-lang_file\fR \fIlang\fR
Set interface language code (\fBen\fR, \fBpt\fR) or path to a JSON language file.
.TP
\fB\-\-log\fR [\fIpath\fR]
Enable command audit logging. If path is omitted, writes to
\fI~/.aethercli/log/aethercli.log\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-adduser\fR \fIname\fR
Interactively add or update credentials for a user in \fBusers.json\fR.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Display command-line options summary and exit.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Display version information and exit.

.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\fR
Model Context Protocol server over stdio. Only in binaries built with
\fB\-DAETHERCLI_ENABLE_MCP=ON\fR. See \fBMCP SERVER\fR.

.SH USE CASE: VALIDATED BACKUP
Operators and AI agents may run backups, but only for a known database and a
retention of 1 to 90 days. In \fIconfig.json\fR:
.PP
.nf
{
  "name": "database",
  "subcommands": [
    {
      "name": "backup",
      "activation": "${AETHERCLI_SCRIPTS_DIR}/backup.sh",
      "timeout_s": 600,
      "params": [
        { "name": "bank", "type": "string", "required": true,
          "enum": ["sales", "stock"] },
        { "name": "retention", "type": "integer", "required": true,
          "minimum": 1, "maximum": 90 }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
.fi
.PP
\fBaethercli \-p "database backup sales 7"\fR runs \fIbackup.sh sales 7\fR and
exits with its status. \fBaethercli \-p "database backup sales 365"\fR is refused
without running anything and exits with status 1. Through \fB\-\-mcp\fR, the same
command is the tool \fBdatabase_backup\fR with a generated input schema.

.SH MCP SERVER
\fBaethercli \-\-mcp\fR reads one JSON-RPC message per line from standard input.
It supports protocol revisions 2026-07-28 (per-request version in
\fBparams._meta\fR, \fBserver/discover\fR) and 2025-11-25 (\fBinitialize\fR).
Commands with an activation become tools named by joining the path with
underscores; \fBinternal:\fR activations are not exposed, nor are \fBadmin_only\fR
commands for other users. Each command gets \fI/dev/null\fR as standard input.
.PP
With \fBrequire_authentication\fR, the server needs valid \fBAETHERCLI_USER\fR and
\fBAETHERCLI_PASSWORD\fR variables and otherwise exits with status 1. The bootstrap
of the interactive login (becoming \fBadmin\fR with no users file) does not apply.

.SH INTERACTIVE NAVIGATION AND SHORTCUTS
.IP \(bu 3
\fBTAB (1x):\fR Autocompletes token up to longest common prefix. Unique matches append a trailing space.
.IP \(bu 3
\fBTAB (2x):\fR Lists available subcommands at current level.
.IP \(bu 3
\fBTAB (3x):\fR Lists options with syntax help.
.IP \(bu 3
\fBQuick Help (?):\fR Typing \fB?\fR outside quotes displays valid options immediately without pressing ENTER.
.IP \(bu 3
\fBUnique Prefixes:\fR Any unambiguous abbreviation is executed as full command (e.g. \fBserv sta\fR runs \fBservice start\fR).
.IP \(bu 3
\fBEditing Keys:\fR \fBCtrl+A\fR (beginning), \fBCtrl+E\fR (end), \fBCtrl+K\fR (kill to end), \fBCtrl+U\fR (kill to start), \fBCtrl+W\fR (delete preceding word), \fBCtrl+L\fR (clear screen).
.IP \(bu 3
\fBHistory:\fR Up and Down arrows recall previous commands. Persisted in \fI~/.aethercli/history\fR (mode \fB0600\fR).
.IP \(bu 3
\fBHot Reload:\fR \fBinternal:reload\fR reloads configuration and translations in memory without restarting terminal session.

.SH EXIT STATUS
\fBaethercli\fR returns the following exit status codes:
.TP
.B 0
Successful termination.
.TP
.B 1
Fatal startup error, authentication failure, or execution abort.
.TP
.B 2
Command-line option syntax error or missing argument.
.TP
.B 124
Command exceeded \fBtimeout_s\fR.
.TP
.B 127
Command not found in operating system, or unknown command.
.TP
.B 128+N
Terminated by UNIX signal \fIN\fR (e.g., 130 for SIGINT/Ctrl+C).

.SH SECURITY
.IP \(bu 3
\fBArgument Escaping:\fR All forwarded arguments are sanitized and escaped to prevent shell injection.
.IP \(bu 3
\fBFlag Injection Prevention:\fR Arguments and parameter values without \fBenum\fR beginning with \fB\-\fR are rejected unless \fBactivation\fR ends with \fB\ \-\-\fR.
.IP \(bu 3
\fBLine Length Cap:\fR Command lines exceeding 4096 bytes are blocked.
.IP \(bu 3
\fBConfiguration File Permissions:\fR Securing \fBconfig.json\fR with root ownership and \fB0644\fR permissions is required to prevent unprivileged privilege escalation.

.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/aethercli
System-wide executable binary.
.TP
.I /usr/local/etc/aethercli/config.json
Default master configuration file.
.TP
.I ~/.aethercli/config.json
Per-user configuration file.
.TP
.I ~/.aethercli/history
Per-user persistent history file.
.TP
.I ~/.aethercli/log/aethercli.log
Default execution audit log file.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B AETHERCLI_CONFIG
Absolute path to currently active configuration file.
.TP
.B AETHERCLI_SCRIPTS_DIR
Path to directory where automation scripts are resolved.
.TP
.B AETHERCLI_USER
Authenticated session username, set for commands. As input, it selects the user
only when authentication is off, or together with \fBAETHERCLI_PASSWORD\fR in MCP mode.
.TP
.B AETHERCLI_PASSWORD
Password for MCP mode. Removed from the environment at startup and not passed to commands.
.TP
.B AETHERCLI_PARAM_\fINAME\fR
Value of a declared parameter, with the name in upper case.
.TP
.B AETHERCLI_LANG
Current language code.
.TP
.B AETHERCLI_PEPPER
Optional secret pepper used during PBKDF2 password verification.

